Short:		Driver for G3/G4 PCI cards
Uploader:	dennisvdboon@gmail.com (Dennis Boon)
Author:		dennisvdboon -> gmail (Dennis Boon)
Type:		driver/other
Version:	17.16
Architecture:	m68k-amigaos = 3.9
Distribution:	Aminet
URL:		https://github.com/Sakura-IT/SonnetAmiga

Slightly less short:

The included powerpc.library is a driver for the Sonnet 7200 G3/G4 PCI card,
the Force PowerPMC-250 G4 PMC card and the PrPMC8xx range of cards.
 
What is needed:
 
- Amiga with Zorro III/PCI slots.
- Elbox Mediator 3000Di/4000Di with 3.3V rail.
- Prometheus PCI Zorro III bridge with FireStorm upgrade (17.14).
- 68040 or better.
- A Radeon 92xx, 3DFX Voodoo3/4/5 or Permedia2 card (prometheus/17.14)
- Warp3D 4.2 for 3D graphics in combination with a 3DFX Voodoo card.
- Sonnet 7200 PCI Card. Supported are MG3-400-512K; MG3-400-1M; MG3-500-1M; 
  MG4-400-1M.
- 5V 168 pins FPM DIMMs 2K Refresh (up to 256 MB in combination with the
  Voodoo3; 128MB with other supported video cards) for the Sonnet.
- Force PowerPMC-250 Card boasting a 450MHz G4 CPU and on-board SDRAM. PMC
  carrier card required.
- Motorola PrPMC8xx cards with Harrier chipset with either G3 or G4 processor
  and 256MB of on-board SDRAM. PMC carrier card required.
- Bigfoot Killer NIC M1 PCI card with 400MHz MPC8343E and 64MB on-board RAM
- Bigfoot Killer NIC K1 PCI card with 333MHz MPC8343E and 64MB on-board RAM
- Bigfoot/Xeno Killer NIC 2100 PCIe card with 400MHz MPC8314E and 128MB
  on-board RAM. This only works partially for Xilinx based Prometheus cards.
  Full support will need a further FirmWare fix.
- pci.library version 13.8 or higher from Elbox (does not support PCIe cards).
- prometheus.library version 4.5 or higher (17.14+).
- AHI for sound. AHI 4.18 recommended.
 
What does it do:
 
The library interfaces between the CPU and the memory on certain PCI cards
which contain PPC CPUs (e.g Sonnet Crescendo 7200 etc.) and the Amiga CPU and
memory. It also offers WarpOS compatible functions.
 
What does this mean:
 
It essentially adds a second CPU to your Amiga through the PCI slot. 
This being any of 400MHz MPC8343E up to 1GHz 7450 G4 CPUs. This CPU can be used
to run (certain) WarpOS software.

Restrictions:
 
The PPC CPU on the PPC PCI card cannot DMA to Amiga memory. Any access to
non-PPC/PCI memory by the PPC is very slow. The library is trying to avoid
this by means of patching the AllocMem() and LoadSeg() functions. This way,
PPC data and code is pushed to the memory residing on the PPC card itself.
This is not 100% watertight and failure of these patches can lead to a DSI/ISI
exception or very slow execution of the PPC program.

Not all functions of the original WarpOS powerpc.library are yet implemented or
will be implemented (for example memory protection). 100% compatibility will
probably be never achieved, mostly due to hardware banging of certain WarpOS
software (e.g. PayBack).

Sound is restricted to AHI. Paula 8 bit DMA is the recommended setting. At this
moment, the SB128 and FM801 drivers sometimes produce a distortion in the sound
output with certain bridges. Paula 8 bit Fast modes have seen some success too.

Albeit that the library supports AltiVec code, this has not been fully tested
as there exist to this moment no WarpOS programs with AltiVec code. That being
said, there is a test dnetc client with AltiVec enabled for WarpOS and the
latest SDL library contains AltiVec code.

Options:

The library reads its options from ENV:sonnet when it starts; save them in
ENVARC:sonnet to keep them. Each value is a single digit. Currently supported
are:

- EnEDOMem (0 or 1): Enable if you have EDO RAM installed. Default = 0.
  Only used up to 17.13b.
- Debug (0-3): Set the level of debug messages. 0 = no messages (default).
- EnMemExc (0 or 1): With 1, Alignment and Data Access (DSI) exceptions go
  to the registered exception handlers first, as on WarpOS; an alignment
  exception that no handler takes crashes the task. With 0, the library
  first emulates the misaligned access or does the 68K memory access itself,
  and the handlers only get what it cannot do. Needed by ReFused. Replaces
  EnAlignExc and EnDAccessExc (up to 17.15). Default = 0.
- DisL2Cache (0 or 1): Disable the L2 cache. Default = 0. Only used up to
  17.13b.
- DisL2Flush (0 or 1): Disables the full flush of the L2 cache. 
  Can speed up things if it doesn't crash. Default = 0.
- DisHunkPatch (0 or 1): Disable automatic pushing first code hunk to FAST RAM.
  Default = 0.
- EnStackPatch (0 or 1): Enable if you want the library to push more 68K data
  to 68K memory like stack and task structures. **EXPERIMENTAL**
  Only used up to 17.13b.
- SetCPUComm (0 or 1): If set will use another method of handling cross-cpu
  signalling. If Shogo hangs, try it with this set. Default = 0.
- NoZeroGuard (0 or 1): If set the kernel does not protect the zero page
  anymore. For badly behaving games/programs (such as Shogo). Default = 0.
  **USE AT OWN RISK!!!**
- SetCMemDiv (0-5): Sets the speed divider of the L2 Cache memory.
  5 = 3, 4 = 2.5, 3 = 2, 2 = 1.5, 1 = 1, 0 = library default (3). Default = 0.
  For example: A Sonnet with speed 500 MHz and setting 5 will run the L2 cache
  at 166 MHz (500/3). No effect on a Harrier card with a 750FX, whose L2
  cache is on the CPU. **USE AT OWN RISK!!!**
- ReportG4asG3 (0 or 1): If set, a G4 is reported as a G3 by GetCPU() and by
  GetInfo() (CPU and PVR), and AltiVec is not enabled. Default = 0.
 
Notes:
 
The included powerpc.library is for PCI PPC cards only and not compatible with
CPU/expansion slot PPC solutions (BPPC, CSPPC etc.).
 
Refer to https://github.com/Sakura-IT/SonnetAmiga or /PowerPCAmiga for the
latest build and the latest documentation regarding compatibility, FAQs and
issues.

See http://amigafun.blogspot.com/search/label/Sonnet for some demonstrations of
the Sonnet card in action.

In the archive:

powerpc.library:	Library that initializes the PPC and handles the
			communication between the system and the PPC card.
			Two different versions are included. The old obsolete
			one which was programmed in assembly and a brand new
			one programmed mostly in C. The main difference is
			speed (5%) and support for the Prometheus PCI bridge.
InitPPC:		Initializes the PPC card using the powerpc.library
GetInfo:		Simple program to show the state of the PPC CPU on the
			PPC card after initialization.
StartPPC:		Simple script if you don't want to put the library in
			the Startup-Sequence.
vdappc:			Program from Frank Wille that disassembles PPC code.
			Main difference with the one already available on the
			net is that this compiled version supports Altivec
			disassembly.
wosdb:			Program from Frank Wille that can trace through PPC
			programs. Main difference with the one already
			available on the net is that this compiled version can
			intercept exceptions and trace from there. Original
			program could only trace from the start of a program.
			Thanks Frank!
tools750x:		Various tools to under/overclock the PPC750FX as found
			on the "Ragnarok" PrPMC800 card. Thanks to Wrangler!
bogomips: 		Meaningless speed test program :-)
			
Also the sources from both versions are included.


The Sonnet or PowerPMC card can only be initialized after the graphics card has
been initialized. This means after C:LoadMonDrvs in the Startup-Sequence. To
use it with WarpDT, it needs to be initialized before AddDataTypes in the
Startup-Sequence. It is however recommended to start the driver from the
workbench using the provided icon or using the provided script. The Harrier
bridge based and Killer NIC cards can initialize their CPUs without the aid of
VGA memory.

-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+

The future:

I consider the library as finished. Bug fixes will happen and maybe add PCIe
card support. Currently it does not work fully on the Prometheus, but maybe it
will on the Mediator MKIV in combination with pci.library 14.0. I do not see a
reason to add memory protection as nothing out there uses it.

I looked at an implementation of Nallepuh for Payback, but someone else can
do that :-)

It was a fun 9 year ride and thanks for your support! :-)


-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+-+

What has changed since 17.15:

ENV options (see above):
- Added ReportG4asG3. EnMemExc replaces EnAlignExc and EnDAccessExc.
  SetCMemDiv was read but did nothing; it works now, and the divider is set
  before the L2 cache is switched on (it was changed while the cache ran).

Scheduler and tasks:
- Task priorities work now: the WarpOS priority scheduler (NEWPRIRULE) with
  NICE values and a time slice per task that scales with the load.
- Exceptions no longer restart the time slice or empty the instruction cache
  unless another task gets the CPU (they were about three times slower on a
  G4).
- The tc_Switch and tc_Launch hooks are called, the per-task CPU usage is
  right, TASKATTR_BAT tasks get the system BATs and TASKATTR_ATOMIC tasks keep
  the CPU until they wait or end, as in WarpOS.
- The helpers that load a BAT write all four of its registers now (the first
  was written four times). ChangeMMU() is still a stub, as in 17.13b.
- SetScheduling() compares SCHED_REACTION as a signed number (-1 gave 20, the
  widest ceiling, and gives 1 now, as in 17.13b and WarpOS). SnoopTask()
  refuses a type other than SNOOP_START and SNOOP_EXIT instead of returning an
  ID for it, as in both.
- GetPPCState() answers PPCSTATEF_APPRUNNING while a task is ready to run (it
  answered APPACTIVE), as in 17.13b. A task that a 68k program starts with its
  first RunPPC() begins with its time slice set, as under WarpOS (it began
  with 0 until the next statistics pass).
- A task that wakes from WaitTime() or from a 68k message goes into the ready
  list by its priority, as under WarpOS; it went behind every ready task of
  the same priority, so it waited a time slice for each of them.
- A task switch cancels an lwarx reservation, and tasks start in AltiVec
  non-Java mode (a denormal ended the task).
- Fixed: task names of 251 characters or more (they froze the machine or wrote
  past a buffer); a failed start of a task's 68k side for want of memory (an
  endless wait; it is tried again every second now).
- Fixed: a crashed task taken off the task list and its port freed twice
  (often halting the machine); a task deleted in WaitTime() left its timer
  behind; a message for a task being removed woke it again.
- Fixed: a failed CreateTaskPPC() left TASKPPCF_CHOWN set; 1 KB of every
  CreateTaskPPC() task was never given back. TASKATTR_NOTIFYMSG works.

Exceptions:
- Exceptions run under the library's MMU set-up and on the kernel's own stack,
  and handlers get the full frame.
- Timed handlers (EXCATTR_TIME) ran up to 20 ms late. XCO_EXCID and XCO_R3 of
  XCONTEXT were swapped.
- A DSI handler can claim a fault, and without EnMemExc also gets the faults
  the 68K memory access cannot serve, as in 17.13b.
- A null or misaligned r3 on an sc exception goes to the registered handlers.
  Super() is recognised by its address, as in WarpOS (any exception with
  0xABADBEEF in r4 gave supervisor mode).
- SMALLCONTEXT handlers: several bugs fixed, among them a write below the
  exception frame that cost the task its link to its 68k mirror (signals from
  the 68k, Ctrl-C included, were lost), and the caller's r13 is kept.
- A SetExceptPPC() handler runs whenever the task has one, with the data
  whatever it holds (it needed non-zero data; a task with data and no handler
  made the kernel call address 0).
- SetExcHandler(): a failed call left a freed handler active, a type the
  library does not handle (EXCF_ALTIVECUNAV alone) stopped the program (it
  gets a handle now, which RemExcHandler() frees), and queued changes could be
  moved while a list function was half done.
- CauseInterrupt() runs the EXCF_INTERRUPT handlers before it returns, as
  under WarpOS; CausePPCInterrupt() from a 68k program works on a Harrier.
- From an exception handler, SetCache(), Signal68K() and a reply to a 68k
  message hung or crashed the PPC; they call the kernel directly now, as
  WarpOS's SetCache does.
- PutMsgPPC(), ReplyMsgPPC() and SignalPPC() can be called from handlers, as
  WarpOS documents. A handler must not read or write Amiga memory.
- A PPC program's load from Amiga memory (through the library's exception
  handler) waited for ever when the 68k did not answer; it is refused after 2
  seconds now, like any memory error.
- The crash report no longer reads or writes the PPC's page zero when no task
  runs: it shows "Kernel Panic!" (it could send the 68k's walk of the library
  list round in circles).

Signals and the 68k mirror task:
- The signal relay between a PPC task and its 68k mirror task was reworked
  (signals were lost or stranded, bit 31 was stripped), and the mirror's
  address is read on the PPC side, as in WarpOS.
- SignalPPC() to a 68k process works. A signal sent during a CreateTaskPPC()
  task's first Run68K() is kept now, and a race in that call that could hang
  the task is fixed.
- A signal pending at RunPPC() was sent twice. A SignalPPC() that hit a task's
  exception signals hung the sender, and a task that signalled them could hang
  if an interrupt came at the wrong moment.
- WaitTime() cleared signals 0-9 as well as its own (TrySemaphorePPC() then
  failed after it had got the semaphore). SetSignalPPC() returned the old
  signals with the changed bits cleared, so SetSignalPPC(0, mask) & mask, the
  usual test for Ctrl-C, was always 0 (as in WarpOS now).
- The process that stands for a PPC task waits for MasterControl's signal
  before it makes its message port, so the port cannot share signal bit 31
  with the task (a signal on it was dropped for the life of the pair).
- A 68k mirror task no longer frees a borrowed current directory lock on exit,
  and 68k tasks no longer lose bits 0 and 1 of tc_Flags.

Semaphores:
- ReleaseSemaphorePPC(), ProcurePPC(), VacatePPC(), TrySemaphorePPC() and
  ObtainSemaphoreSharedPPC() could corrupt a semaphore or its wait queue, lose
  the owner or a count, grant an exclusive request to a semaphore held shared,
  stop a task with "PPC Semaphore in illegal state", leave a semaphore held,
  freeze the PPC, crash when a request was used again, or leave a task asleep
  for good; all fixed.
- The hand-off in ReleaseSemaphorePPC() completes under the lock (WarpOS and
  17.13b have the same window), and ObtainSemaphoreSharedPPC() raises the nest
  count before it releases the lock, which 17.13b and WarpOS do the other way
  round: this release differs from them on purpose there (ObtainSemaphorePPC()
  keeps their order).

RunPPC(), Run68K() and the 68k side:
- RunPPC() and WaitForPPC() hand back the registers and leave the PPCArgs
  structure as it was (a second call with it crashed the PPC), RunPPC() tests
  PPF_ASYNC as a bit, and WaitForPPC() with no call out returns PPERR_WAITERR
  and no longer raises a negative priority to 0, as in WarpOS.
- A function started with RunPPC() gets a 32-byte aligned stack (AltiVec code
  on the stack read wrong bytes), and a failed first RunPPC() leaves nothing
  behind (the next one built a PPC task at a random address).
- Run68K() with PPF_ASYNC works (collected with WaitFor68K()), a missing code
  address returns PPERR_MISCERR, and the 68k function's a6 is stored in the
  right place afterwards.
- The 68k side searches its list of the processes that have used RunPPC() with
  task switching forbidden, as WarpOS does (17.13b searches without a lock): a
  task switched out in the search could follow freed memory (a bus error or a
  Guru) or build a second PPC task for its caller.

Messages, pools and memory calls:
- A long run of PPC writes to Amiga memory, or debug output, could reuse a
  message frame still in use; the PPC waits for a free frame now, and the 68k
  side no longer takes an empty queue for a frame.
- The 68k side no longer freezes the machine, with interrupts off, when it
  reads a message from the PPC twice and finds the queue empty (as in 17.13b).
  ReplyMsgPPC() marks every reply NT_REPLYMSG.
- As in WarpOS: CreateMsgPortPPC() returns NULL and gives back what it took
  when it fails; AllocPooledPPC() and FreePooledPPC() with no pool return at
  once (the program was stopped and every later PPC allocation hung), and
  AllocPooledPPC() returns NULL for a size of 0.
- As in WarpOS: ChangeStack() returns CHSTACK_SUCCESS, a second call works and
  a request smaller than the stack succeeds without a change; RawDoFmtPPC()
  printed every hexadecimal number wrongly, treated a precision as a minimum
  and cut strings of 256 characters or more.
- As in WarpOS: FreeXMsgPPC() and FreeXMsg() hand an XMsg made on the other
  CPU to the side that made it (before: an error or corrupted memory), and a
  PPC XMsg reaches a 68k port as NT_XMSGPPC.
- AllocPooledPPC() for a size close to the puddle size failed and left an
  empty puddle behind on every try; it gets its own block now. FreePooledPPC()
  joins freed memory to the free space below it, as in 17.13b.
- AllocXMsg() counts the 20-byte message header (a body of 64 gave a length of
  64 where AllocXMsgPPC() gives 96), and InsertPPC() with the tail marker of a
  list as the predecessor sets the node's back link (RemovePPC(), RemTailPPC()
  and a walk back from the last node went through a stale link), as 17.13b and
  WarpOS do.

Caches and floating point:
- SetCache() on a card without an L2 (K1, M1) flushes and invalidates the
  range it is given, Run68K() flushes all its stack arguments, flushing the
  whole data cache leaves no modified lines, turning the L2 off with
  DisL2Flush set keeps its data, and a cache switched back on drops its old
  lines; the Killer's L1 caches are cleared at start-up.
- SetCache() on memory the PPC cannot reach (Amiga fast RAM, the ROM) killed
  the task and slowed every later exception, and on chip RAM an invalidate
  could damage the library's memory: it acts only on memory the PPC can cache
  now.
- An L2 whose size cannot be measured at start-up is taken as 1 MB (it was 256
  MB, and a flush of the whole data cache crashed the program).
- The L2 size is found by a data check, largest first (2 MB on a 7400 or 7410,
  then 1 MB, 512 KB and 256 KB); a 2 MB L2 was run as 1 MB. The check runs
  with address translation off, and the L2 is switched off before each
  invalidate.
- GetInfo() reads the cache state, the page table and the L2 from the CPU at
  the call (after a SetCache() it showed the old state), and its L2 tags answer
  on/off as 1 or 0, the write-through state and the size in use, as in 17.13b.
- SetCache(DCACHEOFF) switches an enabled L2 off together with the data cache
  and DCACHEON switches it on again (the manual wants the L2 off whenever the
  data cache is off); an L2CACHEON made while the data cache is off waits for
  DCACHEON.
- SetCache(L2WTON) on an enabled L2 flushes it, switches it off, sets
  write-through, invalidates it and switches it on again (the manual forbids
  setting the bit while the L2 runs).
- Floating-point accesses a G3 or G4 hands to software: a negative
  displacement of a misaligned access is signed now (it went 64 KB too far),
  lfdu, stfdu, lfsux, lfdx, lfdux, stfsux, stfdx, stfdux, stfiwx and dcbz are
  emulated as in WarpOS, and a misaligned lwarx or stwcx. goes to the task's
  exception handlers.
- Indexed loads and stores (lfsx, lfdx, stfsx, stfdx) and lmw and stmw on
  Amiga memory are refused like the other floating-point accesses there (they
  were emulated as integer accesses).

Start-up and cards:
- Harrier: the start-up switched off copy-back snarfing (BXCS bit CSE), which
  hung a G4 (ScummVM, GLQuake), and counted 983,299 interrupt acknowledgements
  where 15 are meant; both fixed.
- Harrier: the PPC memory is measured, not taken from the memory controller's
  setting alone. The setting is read as 64, 128, 256 or 512 MB (unset: 256
  MB), the largest PCI memory that fits is taken, and a word written 64, 128
  and 256 MB up that comes back at the start shows a smaller RAM, so the
  layout is reduced and the setting rewritten.
- A 128 MB Harrier card that got 192 MB of PCI space was mapped as 192 MB (its
  top 64 MB was its bottom 64 MB again), and a 64 MB card was not known;
  17.13b stopped with "PPC memory wrapping error detected. Please reboot!".
- The PCIe M1 (MPC8314E) starts and runs PPC programs. K1/M1 (PCI): the memory
  of a second graphics card (a Radeon, with a Voodoo as primary card) is
  usable by the PPC again, as in 17.13b.
- prometheus.library is tried before pci.library (FireStorm/Firebird first,
  otherwise the Mediator). GetInfo() GETINFO_CPU on Harrier and MPC107 boards
  answers from the CPU itself, as 17.13b (the Harrier knew only a 750FX or
  7410, the MPC107 took a 745x for a G3).
- When the library cannot start (a 68030, another PPC library running, no
  supported PCI bridge, a missing pci.library or prometheus.library) it shows
  one requester and closes only the libraries it opened, each once (it could
  close others, twice, or hang if the ramlib stack could not be enlarged; on
  e300 cards a warning closed libraries still in use); a prometheus.library
  that is too old is not left open.
- The dummy warp.library (now 5.2) is built in PPC memory, so PPC code can
  call it. The NICE table sits in the library's own memory, as in 17.13b.
  SetHardware(HW_BRANCHTRACEOFF) clears the branch trace bit and not the
  single-step bit.
- MPC745x boards: the time base and decrementer are switched on (HID0 TBEN)
  and the on-chip L2 is set up with its own sequence, whose invalidate is
  waited for on L2I (not run on a 745x board).
- K1/M1 (PCI): a frame posted just as the PPC finished reading the queue waited
  for the next interrupt; the interrupt is acknowledged before the queue is
  read now (the PCIe M1 likewise, not run there).
- K1/M1 (PCI): the bus clock and the task slice follow the board's DDRCM
  strap; the reset word was read byte-reversed and the wrong bit tested.
- MPC107: the wait for the PPC's first exception at start-up is bounded (a PPC
  that stopped hung ramlib for good) and ends with "PowerPC CPU stopped after
  setup" (not run on an MPC107 board).

Program loader and process exit:
- With SetCPUComm set, hunks that asked for FAST or CHIP memory are loaded
  into Amiga memory. The loader recognises ppc.library and AmigaAMP, keeps the
  memory of an overlaid program, gives back a file handle (and a FIB) when a
  file cannot be examined, and makes no exception for one build of the Radeon
  driver.
- A process whose exit routine lay in ROM handed dos a return code of 0 and
  left 16 bytes on the stack; it passes the code on and leaves the stack as it
  was, as in 17.13b. The AllocMem() patch is as in 17.13b again (it read
  address 0xFFFFFFFB for a task without a name, missed some task names, turned
  away MEMF_FAST alone and had lost the AmigaAMP case).

What has changed since 17.14:

- Not enough room on the PPC MMU pagetable when Voodoo4/5 installed. Fixed.
- FindSemaphorePPC searched the wrong list. Fixed.
- Double freeing memory for semaphores fixed.
- Added that the exception/crash output also goes to serial.
- Added basic support for Bigfoot/Xeno Killer 2100 PCIe cards.
- Added Altivec support for CopyMemPPC when available. Needs 128bit alignment.
- Added NoZeroGuard as an ENV option. See above.
- Changed description for ENV option SetCPUComm. See above.

What has changed since 17.13b:

- Rewritten all code in C. Startup code is still in assembly.
- Added support for the Prometheus Bridge with FireStorm firmware. Needs
  prometheus.library 4.0 or better (See EAB or contact me).
- WARNING: Timings on the Prometheus seem not so tolerant. K1/M1 cards do not
  work with the Prometheus and Warp3D (Bus errors).  
- Added support for the Permedia2 PCI card (2D only).
- Rewritten memory detection on the Harrier cards. No ENV needed anymore.
  Up to 384MB supported.

What has changed since 17.12:

- Removed left-over debug code.
- Added check on 128MB Harriers.
- Added new ENV called Harrier256MB.
- Fixed 256+MB G3 Harrier support.
- Fixed an Enforcer hit.
- Fixed a possible bug for cards with more than 256MB memory.
- Clarified an error message.
- Added an extra error regarding memory wrapping.
- Fixed an error where the PPC memory size was saved wrongly.
- Fixed PPCBase initialization by adding PPC_SysLib and PPC_DosLib values.
- Added basic support for Mediator MKIII.

What has changed since 17.11:

- Library now requires pci.library 13.8+ from Elbox.
- Added error output when a time-out occurs on 68K calling by the PPC.
- Added more crash error messages (e.g. when PPC is out of memory).
- Page table size now allocated based on total memory on card.
- Fixed Quantum/time-slice not being set correctly by CreateTaskPPC.
- Harrier cards now allocate memory based on available Z3 window space.
- Fixed RemTailPPC returning LH_TAILPRED when list was empty.
- Trying to send a signal to a non-existing 68k mirror task now prevented.
- Data storage exceptions can now be intercepted correctly by wosdb debugger.
- Added $5961 and $5962 to supported ATI Radeon cards.
- Library now keeps track of number of Run68Ks called.
- Updated the GetInfo utility with above info and also added 7457 CPU.
- Added a message when the kernel crashes.
- Added 3.5 and 4.0 multiplier for Rapture 7410 cards.
- Overhauled the signalling system making DisSigBounce obsolete.
- Added a Stricmp workaround for lha_wos 2.14 which apparently sorts using the
  68k utility.library. Archiving now starts a lot faster.
- Added a work-around to speed up WarpDT in combination with picture.datatype
  version 45.17. (WarpDT needs the PPC card to be started from startup-
  sequence).

What has changed since 17.10:

- Added support for Bigfoot Killer NIC K1 PCI cards.
- Added access to Radeon config space for K1/M1 PPC cards.
- Fixed reporting of L2 size for Killer NIC cards
- Added experimental code to add pcidma memory for use by K1/M1 cards. This is
  enabled when a Radeon is added as a secondary card.
- PPC MMU now correctly initialized in case of 128MB(x2) Radeon.
- Harrier based cards can use up to 384MB of local RAM for PPC programs when
  enough room in Z3 space,
- Fixed errors in MMU page table set-up code.
- Fixed register trashing when Killer or Harrier present (code was executed
  using MPC107 register offsets).
- Now the whole 512MB mediator memory range gets mapped to the PPC.
- K1 now has a correct decrementer interrupt going on. The time slice was
  wrongly calculated resulting in occasional stalls of the PPC,


What has changed since 17.9:

- GetCPU() now correctly recognizes a Nitro CPU as a G4.
- Added basic MPC7450 support (Gyrus X2 PMC card).
- Added MPC7441, PPC603E to GetInfo tool.
- Fixed an error in RunPPC that caused screen corruption (e.g. in Earth2140).
- Fixed tearing of screens in some games (e.g. FreeSpace).
- Added support for Bigfoot Killer NIC M1 PCI cards.


What has changed since 17.8:

- Added ENV DisSigBounce. If set signals are less likely to continuously bounce
  between processors. This lessens the load on the PCI bus and can speed up
  certain programs/games. Programs that use a lot of DSI emulations (like
  scummvmwos 0.7) will hang. Turn this setting off when that happens.
- Added Harrier chipset support. This will open up a range of support for PMC
  cards based on this chipset. For now PrPMC800 has been tested and is
  officially supported. Others have had success with the PrPMC8150 card.
- Added more MHz options to the settings table. Up to 1GHz now supported.  

What has changed since 17.7:

- Fixed a memory leak. Stack of a PPC task was not freed.
- LoadSeg() patch crashed on overlaid files. They are now ignored.
- Added more error handling/messages.
- SnoopTask() on exit was not implemented correctly. Fixed.
- SnoopTask() should now work with SNOOP_START.
- Implemented task exceptions from SetExceptPPC().
- Small compatibility update regarding PPCBase (extended libbase).
- Aligned data handling within the library to 16 byte boundaries (for Altivec).
- Fixed a trashing of a segment pointer inside the LoadSeg() patch.
- Extended LargeContext to hold Altivec registers.
- Modules of WarpRace are now loaded correctly.
- Added support for the Force Computers PowerPMC-250 450MHz G4 card,
- Added CPUF_7410 for MPC7410 CPUs.
- Added size detection code for SDRAM to the library.
- Added option sonnet/EnStackPatch.

What has changed since 17.6:

- CauseInterrupt() and CausePPCInterrupt() now work as intended.
- The option to enable a DSI exception handler now works as intended.
- Removed a lot of special purpose register (SPRGx) usage.
- Made several fixes to the init code when a Radeon is present.
- Loading DefIcons no longers goes into a infinite loop due to LoadSeg() patch.
- Added the ENV variable SetCMemDiv. Now you can set the speed of the L2 cache.
- First hunk of WarpOS programs should now correctly be loaded to FAST RAM.
- Made some tweaks to the AllocMem() patch.
- Cybermand and Cyberpi now work correctly with hunk patch.
- Fixed a memory leak. Stack of deleted PPC tasks was not freed.
- LoadSeg() patch crashed on overlaid files. These are now ignored.


What has changed since 17.5:

- Fully working PP_STACKPTR and PP_STACKSIZE for Run68K().
- Fixed typo in GetPPCState().
- Added more error messages.
- Fixed a bug in the scheduler. Removed tasks were not handled properly.
- Added a memory clean-up task. Memory of removed tasks is now freed properly.
- Added options through ENVARC:sonnet
- Kernel now removes crashed tasks and continues instead of halting.
- Length of context-switch structure was incorrect.
- Completely rewrote all the exception handlers.
- Added DSI, Decrementer and External Interrupt exception hooks.
- Fixed an error in the custom handler code in case of small context used.
- Register r3 was trashed when more than 1 custom handler present. Fixed.
- Added option to force first hunk of WarpOS program to FAST memory by default.
- LoadSeg patch now correctly handles bit 30 of HUNK_SIZE (move to CHIP).
- Sometimes PPC code was loaded into FAST RAM through the LoadSeg patch. Fixed.
- Mediator config jumper can now be in ANY position for the sonnet to work.
- AllocMem() patch now correctly handles CLI names.
- FreeSpace movies now play at full speed.

What has changed since 17.4:

- Fixed a bug in the FPU opcode detection in the Alignment handler.
- Fixed various Enforcer hits on the 68K side.
- Stack transfer limit 68K->PPC of 164 bytes removed (RunPPC).
- Basic stack transfer support added to Run68K().
- Added support for Radeon with VendorID $5964.
- Added a PPC memory test. This is run during memory initialization.
- Added a timeout for the PPC to report back during initialization.
- Added checks on the SwapConfig and WindowSize jumpers of the mediator.
- Added experimental AltiVec support.
- Added powerpc.library to distribution. See notes above.

What has changed since 17.3:

- Fixed a few more potential hangs with well placed syncs.
- GetInfo() now recognizes modded 433/466/533 MHz Cards.
- Z flag of 68K interrupt now handled correctly upon exit interrupt.
- User code no longer addresses the zero page. That is now Supervisor only.
- Kernel and graphics memory are now handled by BATs by default.
- L2 cache can now cache instructions, not only data.
- L2 cache now set to 200MHz for 400/1MB version instead of 133MHz.
- Fixed a bug in GetHALInfo().
- Fixed a bug where inter-CPU messages were duplicated.
- Inter-CPU messages can now be up to ~64K in size instead of 150 bytes.
- Other miscelaneous fixes to increase stabilty and speed.

What has changed since 17.0:

- Moved all patches inside the library, no more different start-up programs.
- Implemented PPF_ASYNC/PP_STACKPTR/PP_STACKSIZE for RunPPC().
- Fixed a bug in CreatePPCTask(). Now APDF works.
- Added better error handling through pop-up windows/requesters.
- Added a ramlib patch to increase stack when needed.
- Fixed the TagItem functions. Now more demos from Mad Wizards started working.
- Fixed a bug in GetInfo that was masked by the bug in the TagItem functions.
- Added shared signal system. Now the signals are shared by the 2 processors.
- Fixed LockTaskList function. Wrong result was returned.
- Added CPUUSage, Busy, Activity, CPU Load and System load code (for WarpStat).
- Fixed a bug in DeleteTaskPPC() as the Snoop list was not correctly freed.
- Fixed a bug in PutXMsg(). WES should work now (albeit slow).
- Added alignment support to AllocVecPPC(); For iFusion..No that does not work.
- Fixed a missing opcode in the FPU alignment emulation code. Now Q1A works.
- Other miscelaneous fixes and optimizations.

